---
import LetsTalk from '@components/home/LetsTalk.astro'
import Insight from '@components/insights/Insight.astro'
import InsightSignoff from '@components/insights/InsightSignoff.astro'
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro'
import { getInsightNav } from '@data/insights'
import InsightNav from '@components/insights/InsightNav.astro'

const { previous, current, next } = getInsightNav(
  'web-consultant-versus-in-house-developer-the-trade-offs-for-australian-businesses'
)
---

<Layout title={current?.title} description={current?.summary}>
  <Insight tags={current?.tags}>
    <span slot="insight-title">{current?.title}</span>
    <span slot="insight-subtitle">{current?.dateLabel}</span>
    <span slot="insight-text">
      <p>
        At some point the invoices from your agency or consultant add up to more than a salary, and hiring someone
        in-house starts to look obvious. Sometimes it is. Here is what to weigh before you decide:
      </p><br />
      <ul class="lg:mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>The cost line is the wrong place to start.</strong> If the work is continuous, only needs one
            discipline, and someone inside your business can scope and manage it, an in-house developer makes sense. If any
            of those three is missing, the salary comparison is misleading.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>Digital work is rarely one discipline.</strong> A website or app needs front-end and back-end
            development, design, testing and someone to manage it. One hire covers one of those. A consultant or agency
            gives you the rest as you need them, without five salaries.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>Someone still has to manage the work.</strong> If nobody in the business can gather requirements, set
            expectations, estimate and prioritise, that job lands on the developer. Some people can carry it. Most developers
            were hired to build, and every hour spent managing stakeholders is an hour not building.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>You may not know a good developer when you see one.</strong> Interviewing technical people without
            technical people on your side is a coin toss. A bad hire costs you the salary, the recruitment fee, the months of
            low output, and the cleanup after they leave.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>The salary is not the whole cost.</strong> Add superannuation, leave, equipment, tooling, recruitment and
            the ramp-up period before they know your systems. Then add the gap when they resign.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>Consultants have their own overheads.</strong> Every engagement starts with getting up to speed on your
            systems and processes, and some context is lost between projects. That is real time you pay for.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>The quick sanity check is worth more than it looks.</strong> A consultant who has seen a hundred
            businesses will often stop you going down an expensive wrong path in a five-minute conversation. An in-house
            developer, however good, has usually only seen yours.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>You can do both.</strong> An in-house developer for the continuous work and a consultant for direction,
            architecture and the occasional sanity check is a common arrangement. As your team grows, the consultant becomes
            unnecessary, which is how it should go.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <br />
      <p>
        There is no answer that fits every business. We have told clients to hire a developer and stop paying us, and we
        have told clients not to hire because nobody inside could direct the work. If you are not sure which situation you
        are in, that conversation is what our
        <a href="/services/consulting" class="underline underline-offset-2">consulting</a> is for.
      </p><br />
      <InsightSignoff />
    </span>
  </Insight>

  <InsightNav previousInsight={previous} nextInsight={next} />

  <LetsTalk config={{ buttons: 'both', primaryCta: 'message' }} />
</Layout>
